    "md5": "ddb1c63e0bbf3784f245b9c7c1bbcf2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1802,
    "chunk_text": "One of the biggest problems Uthpala faced was the English language. Back at his first school in Mataluwawa, English had been taught as a subject for a few periods a week. In Colombo, by contrast, he came across it every day, indeed almost every hour. Complicating matters further, his new school had students from diverse backgrounds. For many of these students, English was a first language. Among them were Uthpala\u2019s classmates.\n\nBy the time he did his Advanced Level exams in 2022, he had progressed far. While speaking in English remained a challenge, he was confident enough to read and write. He wrote his exams in his language, Sinhala, but compensated for this by reading as much as possible in English. Moreover, at the time of his final exams, he had accomplished much as a student leader. He had become a member, chairman, or secretary of numerous clubs and societies. Among these, not surprisingly, was the College history club. In 2022 he had also served as the Head Prefect of his school\u2019s hostel, putting together events in record time and ensuring the welfare of the boarders during a severe economic crisis.\n\nUthpala finished his exams in January 2023. By then he had applied for the seniormost leadership position at school, that of senior prefect. A month later his application was approved and he was appointed, together with several of his friends.\n\nIn many ways, Uthpala felt like a fish out of water. Many of his peers were from privileged, Westernized, and highly sophisticated backgrounds. He was a product of a very different environment. Though he had reached the top, there was still a gulf between his world and theirs. Perhaps it was this which caught my eye. Whatever the reason, in 2023, when I began my research career, it was Uthpala who became my first assistant."
  },
