    "md5": "501fba6e4d62cc74d59d25b997bd52a2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1557,
    "chunk_text": "It is this which ultimately informed his project, which is simply titled Five Sacred Sites. As the title makes clear, it explores the history of five sites. Two of these are in Kurunegala, in the northwestern province, while three are in the north.\n\nWhen pondering his choices, Uthpala notes that he wanted to focus on sites far away from locations that scholars of Sri Lankan history usually focus on.\n\nThis is a point which resonates deeply with my own work. As renowned historians like Sujit Sivasunderam and Nira Wickramasinghe have written, Sri Lanka\u2019s past tends to be framed in line with colonial notions of history. In the early 20th century, for instance, Sri Lanka, then a British colony, witnessed numerous cultural revivals. These led to a consolidation of ethnic identity that, in the longer term, fueled tensions between certain communities. Reflecting on this, Uthpala says, \u201cThe result is that even \u2018value-neutral\u2019 subjects like history have been weaponized in the service of exclusivist ideologies.\u201d\n\nWhat interests me more than these ideas, however, is Uthpala\u2019s own transformation. While in Kurunegala and even in Colombo, he says he subscribed to certain interpretations of history \u201cbecause they were inscribed in stone and we were supposed to accept them.\u201d What is true of Sri Lanka in this respect is, I think, true of many other countries in South Asia, indeed the Global South. The tendency to associate a country or a society with one group, to the exclusion of others, starts in the classroom and with the textbook."
  },
