    "chunk_text": "Review on The Ceylon Journal Volume 2 Number 2\n\nThe Ceylon Journal\u2019s fourth installment (Vol. 2 No. 2) was published at a poignant moment in Sri Lankan history. The Cyclone Ditwah  has caused the demise of 486 Sri Lankans with over a million affected as of now. This was the first time in our history that all four main rivers have overflown simultaneously. The locals have shown their resilience and the Lankan diaspora has stood in solidarity with the unfortunate souls. Things have been at a standstill. However, the team behind The Ceylon Journal have continued with unabated momentum. Credit to where it\u2019s due.\n\nThe team comprises of some of Sri Lanka\u2019s finest in their diverse fields. It is a testament to their collective goodwill and cooperation that The Ceylon Journal has got off the mark and is flourishing. An increased circulation is evidence to it. The Patron Desamanya M.D.D.Peiris and the Editor-in-Chief Avishka Senewiratne are truly a dynamic duo.\n\nAvishka is no stranger to any reader of the Ceylon Journal. His works have been read in at least four continents. From an amateur who started off his literary career by writing about his school he has blossomed into a bona fide multifaceted writer. He is now an acclaimed writer on matters ranging from the history of our Island nation, its colonial times, political evolution and the arts. He has ventured into the field of the Press and Demographics lately. This writer is fortunate to be acquainted with Senewiratne. They collaborated in bringing to you the story of how the Sri Lankan parliament was shifted from Galle Face to the present location at Sri Jayewardenepura Kotte in the last publication of The Ceylon Journal (Volume 2 Number 1).\n\nThe latest volume of magazine has once again done its readers proud. Those who shall get their hands on the copies will gather a wealth of knowledge encompassing aviation, Buddhism, flora and fauna, sociology, demographics, archaeology, public transport et al."
